SUBJECT. ONE single collectible medal: a struck, enamelled metal plate, and nothing else.

PLATE. The plate is cut into a regular PENTAGON standing on its flat bottom edge with a single point at the top. Its face is filled edge to edge with deep wine burgundy enamel, glassy and deep, the colour of a cabinet's velvet lining. That enamel field is completely SMOOTH and PLAIN: it may have glassy depth, a soft reflection and a slight value shift, but it carries no pattern, no sunburst, no radiating stripes, no carved scrollwork and no filigree of any kind.

FRAME. The raised rim around the plate is made of PALE SILVER under a PRISMATIC CRYSTAL GLAZE: the frame is cut from a clear faceted mineral laid over pale metal, and an iridescent sheen of cyan, rose and pale gold slides across it as if it were a rare foil card. Never a flat rainbow stripe: an iridescence that follows the facets. ONE band running all the way around the plate, cut into EXACTLY EIGHT flat crystal FACETS so the frame catches the light in eight separate planes. The outermost edge carries the same fine milled (reeded) coin texture. No rivets, no gem.

MOTIF. A three tier stepped display plinth, every step lined with small standing forms, with ONE larger figure crowning the top step. It is rendered in cream ivory and pale stone with only small accents of the frame's own metal, never in the frame's metal as its body, so it separates cleanly from the enamel behind it. It sits raised off the field with its own bevel and its own drop of ambient occlusion under it.

LIGHT. A CLEAR GLOW: the motif is lit from within with a cool white light strong enough to spill onto the enamel and onto the inner face of the frame around it, and the prismatic frame catches that light in its facets. The glow may light the medal's own surfaces and it may NEVER leave the outer contour of the plate. No bloom, no haze, no rays, no fog, no halo outside the plate.

